# The memory/CPU budget for work that is allowed to be slow but not allowed to
# take the machine down: Blender renders, agents driving one, long compiles.
#
# WHY A SLICE AND NOT A WATCHDOG
#  2026-11-18: an agent rendered a Blender scene with Cycles set to CPU. The
#  render filled RAM and zram (this machine's only swap is compressed RAM), and
#  the kernel spent its time in reclaim instead of killing anything -- the
#  desktop froze and the machine had to be restarted.
#
#  A limit has to bite *before* that point, which means the kernel's own cgroup
#  accounting, not a sampler hoping to notice in time. Inside this slice the
#  memory controller does the work: past MemoryHigh the kernel throttles and
#  reclaims, past MemoryMax it OOM-kills the biggest process *in the slice*, and
#  the desktop outside it is untouched. memory-guard (see its script header) is
#  the backstop for anything started outside this budget.
#
# NUMBERS, for 32 GB of RAM and 32 threads
#   MemoryHigh  45%  ~14 GB  where reclaim starts to push back
#   MemoryMax   60%  ~19 GB  the hard ceiling: one render cannot have more
#   MemorySwapMax 4 GB       zram is RAM; let heavy work spill a little, not a lot
#   CPUQuota    1600%        leaves 16 of 32 threads for the desktop and for the
#                            input latency that makes a UI feel alive
#
#  Percentages are relative to the parent slice (the user session), so they
#  follow the machine rather than pinning a number that a RAM upgrade would make
#  wrong.
#
# USING IT
#   heavy-run blender -b scene.blend -a          (extras/bin/heavy-run)
#   systemd-run --user --scope --slice=heavy.slice -- make -j32
#   The Blender desktop entry is wrapped in heavy-run, so a GUI session is
#   inside the budget too, which is what the agent that crashed the machine used.
[Unit]
Description=Memory and CPU budget for heavy work (renders, agents, compiles)

[Slice]
MemoryHigh=45%
MemoryMax=60%
MemorySwapMax=4G
CPUQuota=1600%
IOWeight=50
